## Summary
This article examines how shifting geopolitics in Northeast Asia have affected Russia's strategic position. It highlights Japan's rupture with Moscow and North Korea's emergence as a key Russian ally, arguing that both developments reflect how US power is reshaping the regional security order.

## Key Points
- Japan has broken off its relationship with Moscow, marking a significant rupture in bilateral ties
- North Korea has risen as an important ally of Russia
- US power is actively reshaping the security order across Northeast Asia
- Russia has drawn strategic lessons from these geopolitical shifts in Asia


